93 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284263 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284263 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186283513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284263 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b8ed0cd9-02bd-5986-85dc-65dabf690efc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186281776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186281777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186281919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284284 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284284 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284284 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186281777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284284 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c5a40b87-497b-52fb-93f4-bce3fac9446e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284348 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284348 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284348 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186283032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284348 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ebbb4b19-fdb5-5b6d-a6cf-ba581d730234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284417 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284417 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284417 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284417 #edb/ref 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ what type of point is $a?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186284417 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9c2261a-709f-5777-b889-b197f8b76cee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284417 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367743 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367743 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367743 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367743 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367743 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367743 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367743 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921863677